<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Liang (Li Gui)</h1><p class="st-meta">617 CE – 619 CE · capital Wuwei</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A brief kingdom in the Hexi Corridor, centred on Wuwei, set up as Sui collapsed and overthrown in 619 by a Sogdian family who handed it to the Tang.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-gui">Li Gui</a>, a wealthy and respected notable of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Wuwei</a> (Liangzhou), was chosen by local leaders in 617 to resist the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xue-ju">Xue Ju</a>. He proclaimed himself Prince of Great <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/liang-liang-shidu">Liang</a> and in 618 took the title of emperor, controlling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a> oasis cities.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The state sat astride the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>, and Sogdian merchant families were powerful in its capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-gui">Li Gui</a> at first exchanged friendly letters with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozu-of-tang">Li Yuan</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a>, but refused to accept a subordinate title, insisting on equal imperial status.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 619 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> envoy An Xinggui, whose Sogdian family was influential in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Wuwei</a>, worked with his brother An Xiuren to raise troops against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-gui">Li Gui</a>. They captured him and delivered him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, where he was executed, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a> passed to Tang rule.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 617 CE – 619 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3